\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {the hydrogen atom driven by the lattice photon.} A three-dimensional hydrogen atom coupled to the same discrete massless field that carries the graviton. Left: the electron cloud \(|\psi |^2\) and its isosurface --- spherical \(1s\), dumbbell \(2p\), torus \(3d\); right: the mid-plane cut. Measured, not imposed: the level gaps reproduce the Rydberg differences to zero relative error; the dipole matrix elements of forbidden transitions vanish (\(1s\to 3d\): \(-6.8\times 10^{-19}\), \(2p\to 3p\): \(8.6\times 10^{-18}\), against \(9.93\), \(-3.00\), \(22.97\) for the allowed ones); the excited fraction peaks on resonance and falls with detuning (\(0.42\), \(0.35\), \(0.15\), \(0.035\), \(0.011\) at detunings \(0\), \(0.063\), \(0.125\), \(0.25\), \(0.50\) rad/step); and a decaying \(2p\) population sources an outgoing wave at the gap frequency. \textbf  {Provenance of Fig.~\ref {fig:26}.} The atom is an \textbf  {input}: its levels and eigenfunctions are the real hydrogen eigenstates, not derived from the root. What the root supplies is the photon --- its propagation, its resonant absorption, its re-emission. The figure demonstrates the photon sector; it is not a derivation of hydrogen. Two limits are stated rather than hidden: off-resonant absorption is not zero (the Fermi-golden-rule rate has a Lorentzian tail), and a wave packet wider than the simulation box never leaves it, becoming a persistent bath that drives the atom off resonance --- measured at half-gap detuning in a 120-cell box as excited fractions \(0.037\), \(0.135\), \(0.326\) for packet widths of 12, 24, 48 cells. The published figure uses a localised packet (20 cells in a 296-cell box).}}{21}{figure.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:26}{{24}{21}{\textbf {the hydrogen atom driven by the lattice photon.} A three-dimensional hydrogen atom coupled to the same discrete massless field that carries the graviton. Left: the electron cloud \(|\psi |^2\) and its isosurface --- spherical \(1s\), dumbbell \(2p\), torus \(3d\); right: the mid-plane cut. Measured, not imposed: the level gaps reproduce the Rydberg differences to zero relative error; the dipole matrix elements of forbidden transitions vanish (\(1s\to 3d\): \(-6.8\times 10^{-19}\), \(2p\to 3p\): \(8.6\times 10^{-18}\), against \(9.93\), \(-3.00\), \(22.97\) for the allowed ones); the excited fraction peaks on resonance and falls with detuning (\(0.42\), \(0.35\), \(0.15\), \(0.035\), \(0.011\) at detunings \(0\), \(0.063\), \(0.125\), \(0.25\), \(0.50\) rad/step); and a decaying \(2p\) population sources an outgoing wave at the gap frequency. \textbf {Provenance of Fig.~\ref {fig:26}.} The atom is an \textbf {input}: its levels and eigenfunctions are the real hydrogen eigenstates, not derived from the root. What the root supplies is the photon --- its propagation, its resonant absorption, its re-emission. The figure demonstrates the photon sector; it is not a derivation of hydrogen. Two limits are stated rather than hidden: off-resonant absorption is not zero (the Fermi-golden-rule rate has a Lorentzian tail), and a wave packet wider than the simulation box never leaves it, becoming a persistent bath that drives the atom off resonance --- measured at half-gap detuning in a 120-cell box as excited fractions \(0.037\), \(0.135\), \(0.326\) for packet widths of 12, 24, 48 cells. The published figure uses a localised packet (20 cells in a 296-cell box)}{figure.24}{}}
