\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {the baryon-asymmetry product.} \(\Delta B = (28/79)(g_0^4/16\pi )(1/16\pi )^2 = 6.8\times 10^{-10}\) against the measured \(6.1\times 10^{-10}\). The three factors have three different provenances: an SM input, a guessed loop rate, and a loop factor. The automaton alone gives zero.}}{22}{figure.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:8}{{25}{22}{\textbf {the baryon-asymmetry product.} \(\Delta B = (28/79)(g_0^4/16\pi )(1/16\pi )^2 = 6.8\times 10^{-10}\) against the measured \(6.1\times 10^{-10}\). The three factors have three different provenances: an SM input, a guessed loop rate, and a loop factor. The automaton alone gives zero}{figure.25}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {the residual-set reduction.} (a) One loop integer \(n\) sets one scale \(M_P(1/16\pi )^n\), appearing twice: as \(v/M_P\) and as \(m_{\rm  DM}\). These are one equation, \(v = 2.08\,m_{\rm  DM}\), not two independent confirmations. (b) The scan \(n = 1\dots  20\): \(n = 10\) is nearest, \(n = 9\) is \(1.4\) decades away. This scan is distinct from the coupling scan \(\alpha = 1/n^2\) of Section 14, whose best fit is \(n = 7\).}}{23}{figure.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:18}{{26}{23}{\textbf {the residual-set reduction.} (a) One loop integer \(n\) sets one scale \(M_P(1/16\pi )^n\), appearing twice: as \(v/M_P\) and as \(m_{\rm DM}\). These are one equation, \(v = 2.08\,m_{\rm DM}\), not two independent confirmations. (b) The scan \(n = 1\dots 20\): \(n = 10\) is nearest, \(n = 9\) is \(1.4\) decades away. This scan is distinct from the coupling scan \(\alpha = 1/n^2\) of Section 14, whose best fit is \(n = 7\)}{figure.26}{}}
